WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.850
Richard Gere: …Nice…I’ve got new tea…I’m
really happy.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.180
I’m sitting with my friend.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.540
Film Courage: You both have the same month
birthdates which I find very interesting…August

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.540
31st?

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.540
Joseph Cedar: We do.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.870
Richard Gere: Why did I know that?

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:15.500
Film Courage: Oh…you didn’t know that?

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:16.610
Joseph Cedar: I knew that.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.450
Richard Gere: I didn’t know it?

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:20.450
You never told me that!

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:21.450
Joseph Cedar: August 31st.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.890
Richard Gere: This is really bizarre.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:28.050
This explains so much…because we’re not
really Virgos.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.509
Film Courage: You’re not…you’re on the
cusp?

00:00:29.509 --> 00:00:33.430
Richard Gere: Well I don’t really know what
the mix is but we’re not like a normal…he’s

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:34.980
more like a normal Virgo than I am.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:35.980
Film Courage: Okay?

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.760
Joseph Cedar: I know nothing about that…but
I’m happy to share the birthdate…

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:40.760
Richard Gere: I didn’t know that?

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.530
Film Courage: I’m wondering if the two of
you are simpatico?

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:45.610
This was actually going to be my last question
but let’s do it now.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:52.890
I mean…if you both have the same birthdate…there
must be some similar sensibilities or traits?

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:53.890
Maybe not?

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:54.890
I’m reading too much into this?

00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:02.790
Joseph Cedar: …I think as far as this film
(NORMAN) goes…I’m Richard Gere’s inner

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:03.790
“Norman.”

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:11.440
So if there is an inner Norman in Richard
Gere, some of it is on this side of the shot.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:18.001
Richard Gere: There is no question that the
8 or 9 months which we spent together, there

00:01:18.001 --> 00:01:24.280
was a point where you had revealed your inner
Norman enough to me that I went “Aha!

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.740
That’s where I’m going with this.”

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:32.960
Joseph Cedar: But you know I do have to say,
just for the record (between us)…

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.780
Film Courage: Please… Joseph Cedar: I think
part of the fun of working with Norman is

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.760
that Norman goes further than what I ever
would.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.990
So there might be something of Norman in me
but I hold it back.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.780
In the movie, he just lets go.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:47.780
Which is fun.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:48.780
Richard Gere: It’s great fun.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:54.400
Then you were also generous enough with me
that you allowed that inner Norman to emerge

00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.630
and scream once and awhile so I could see
how far I could go with this.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:01.630
Joseph Cedar: Right.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.500
Film Courage: [For Joseph] Well I’m fascinated
by your take on watching Richard change into

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.500
this character.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.090
This is not a normal character for Richard
to play.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.090
He usually plays the alpha male.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.319
Richard Gere: This is not a normal character
for anyone.

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:18.000
I mean this is a completely unique character
and for sure…I mean it’s a big distance

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.279
for me to play him and I think in reality
a big distance for anyone to play this guy.

00:02:23.279 --> 00:02:26.049
He’s written a completely unique character.

00:02:26.049 --> 00:02:31.610
Film Courage: How was it to watch him (Richard
Gere) transform in terms of body language,

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:36.319
the way he carried himself, his interactions
with others, his personal space?

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:43.010
Joseph Cedar: I think that the biggest satisfaction
that I have is when each scene has what the

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.559
scene is about, but then there is this little
nuance that has to do with what the character

00:02:46.559 --> 00:02:52.549
is really going through and it usually has
to do with his…this motivation that he has

00:02:52.549 --> 00:02:53.549
and no one else knows.

00:02:53.549 --> 00:02:54.879
He’s trying to get close to someone.

00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:56.969
He’s trying to introduce someone to someone.

00:02:56.969 --> 00:02:59.239
It’s not the obvious thing.

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:06.150
And seeing Richard grab onto that small nuance
and bring it to life and express it in some

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:11.499
way was incredibly satisfying to me because
it was as if he was revealing this secret

00:03:11.499 --> 00:03:13.680
about what really goes through Norman’s
head.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.209
Richard Gere: I remember him (Joseph) directing
me…I’m flashing on one of the early scenes,

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:24.709
it’s actually the first scene that is in
the movie and I’m approaching Dan Stevens

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:27.430
in the park (Bill Kavish in NORMAN) and I’ve
got a [real estate] deal and I’m trying

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:28.430
to convince him.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:33.980
I remember we were doing the shot where he
moves where he…no one wants anything to

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.640
do with this Norman or any “Norman.”

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:45.430
It’s…how do you make them stop without
losing your own dignity in making them stop?

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:50.769
And you see this a lot with the characters
in this movie who they don’t want to have

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:52.190
to say “Go away.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:53.370
Leave me alone.”

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:56.730
It’s some level of “Please.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.199
Enough!”

00:03:58.199 --> 00:04:03.180
So [in the scene] I’m chasing…I’m chasing
and we’re doing a Steadicam shot and he’s

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:07.709
saying (I remember this direction was like)
“Maybe you can just kind of bob behind him

00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:10.519
and try to get his attention while you’re
coming behind?”

00:04:10.519 --> 00:04:16.280
and it was so…I mean I got it immediately
what you were talking about and that ended

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:19.760
up informing much of what I did in the film.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:20.760
This sense of…

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.750
Joseph Cedar: Right…peaking over someone’s
shoulder and just waiving “I’m still here!”

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.940
is…seeing Richard Gere do that is incredibly
satisfying.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:30.490
Film Courage: Great!

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:31.520
Thank you.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:32.550
That’s excellent.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:33.580
Thank you!

